@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Why SOMA Is Different";
}

<h1 class="display-5 mb-3">Why SOMA Is Different</h1>

<section class="mb-4">
    <h2 class="h4">Horror Without Combat</h2>
    <p>
        Because there is no combat system, you cannot fight back. Stealth, awareness, and puzzle-solving
        are your only tools, which keeps the player vulnerable throughout.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-4">
    <h2 class="h4">Psychological and Existential Dread</h2>
    <p>
        Beyond traditional scares, SOMA is highly regarded for its psychological dread and philosophical
        narrative. It uses its terrifying underwater setting as a vessel for existential horror, probing
        the boundaries of consciousness and identity.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-4">
    <h2 class="h4">Copied, Not Transferred</h2>
    <p>
        The game forces players to confront the reality that human minds in this world are being
        <em>copied</em>, not transferred. The ending illustrates the "coin toss" fallacy of digital cloning.
        It leaves players to agonize over the fate of the versions of ourselves we leave behind.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="mb-4">
    <h2 class="h4">A Question That Stays With You</h2>
    <p>
        Can a digital facsimile of a mind ever truly be considered alive? SOMA does not hand you an
        answer. That is what sets it apart from games built mainly around fear or action.
    </p>
</section>
